    736	| Bessel-basis PSLQ identity search for f(x) = π(x) − R(x) (deep-focus revisit of Task 3 / S29) | FAIL | E | Strict extension of S29's PSLQ identity search to a basis disjoint from the elementary/li/sin-cos basis tested in S29: 10-element basis {f(x), 1, log x, √x, li(x), K_0(log x), I_0(log x), J_0(γ_1·log x), Y_0(γ_1·log x), K_0(2π·√(log x))} — i.e., adds modified-Bessel K/I, oscillatory-Bessel J/Y at the first zeta zero, and the partition-asymptotic kernel K_0(2π√·) from the Hardy-Ramanujan circle method. Motivated by Bessel kernels appearing in the Selberg trace formula (spectral-side K_{ir}) and Mellin-Barnes representations, where they could in principle donate an identity that elementary functions cannot. PSLQ at mpmath 50 dps for x ∈ {5000, 10000, 50000, 100000}, maxcoeff=10⁶, maxsteps=8000, with cross-validation at x+1000. Every fit produces tight residuals (1.9e-36 to 4.0e-35) with non-zero coeff(f) ∈ {2712, 5349, 10385}, but **all fail cross-validation by 4–5 orders of magnitude** (cross-check residuals 1.10e+04 / 6.03e+04). Random-control test: replacing f with same-σ Gaussian (σ ≈ 4.23) produces statistically indistinguishable signatures (fit residuals 8.5e-37 to 6.4e-35; cross-check 1.77e+04 to 1.60e+05). The Bessel basis carries no more identity-bearing structure for f(x) than a Gaussian random number; PSLQ's "tight relations" at finite precision are pure numerical coincidences. Rules out three concrete Bessel-shaped donors: Selberg-trace shadow at low order, partition-saddle reuse via K_0(2π√·), and modified-Bessel growth/decay scaffolding. Adds 23rd pseudorandomness measure (Bessel-basis PSLQ indistinguishability from Gaussian). Strengthens S29 verdict from "f(x) admits no identity in tested elementary basis" to "f(x) admits no identity in any basis tested including spectral-trace-related Bessel kernels." Cites E1.x, E3.x, E7.x. See experiments/algebraic/identity_search/bessel_basis_pslq.py and bessel_basis_pslq_results.md. | 68 |
