    6	---
    7	
    8	## Mode C: Circularity (~60 approaches)
    9	
    10	**Definition:** The method requires knowing primes to compute primes.
    11	
    12	**Examples:**
    13	- Mills' constant: the constant itself encodes all primes
    14	- Generating functions: need primes to evaluate the EGF/OGF
    15	- Chebotarev density: need p to compute Frobenius at p
    16	- Euler product inversion: product is over primes
    17	- Modular forms: Euler factors are indexed by primes
    18	- Graph spectrum approaches: graph construction needs primes
    19	- Expander graphs: Ramanujan graphs built from primes
    20	
    21	**Pattern:** The formula or structure LOOKS like it avoids primes, but
    22	some intermediate step requires prime input. The circularity is often
    23	hidden behind several layers of abstraction.
    24	
    25	**Detection:** Check whether any step requires evaluating a function AT
    26	or OVER primes. If yes, circularity is present.
