    | Regular continued fraction of prime constant α=Σ_{p prime} 2^{-p} (S50-fresh / S51 crit) | FAIL | E | First explicit CF measurement on the binary-prime real constant (vs prior CF tests on p(n)/n at line 615). 1500 partial quotients computed at mpmath 16800-bit precision from 12000 primes. K₀ = 2.7336 vs Khintchine 2.6854 (1.8%); Lévy 1.2056 vs 1.1866 (1.6%); Gauss-Kuzmin probabilities P(a_n=k) match within 0.018 for k=1..3; max autocorrelation lag 1..10 = 0.013; 1/f^β slope = −0.091 (white); k-automaticity inter/intra-variance ratio < 0.012 for k ∈ {2,3,5,7,10}. Switching binary-digit representation to regular CF preserves Shannon entropy (3.26 bits/quotient bucketed) without exposing compressible structure — Khintchine-typical in every measure tested. Adjacent to lines 288, 298, 588, 615; adds CF-of-α as 33rd pseudorandomness measure to novel/pseudorandomness_of_pi.md. See experiments/proposals/proposal_b_cf_prime_constant.py | 51 |
    | Brandt 2024 (TCC) MKtP diagonalisation → π(x) mod 2 lower bound (FOCUS-3) | FAIL | E | Brandt 2024 "Lower Bounds for Levin-Kolmogorov Complexity" (TCC, IACR ePrint 2024/687) proves MKtP ∉ DTIME[O(n)] unconditionally (Thm 1) plus three sharper conditional results (Thm 2 one-sided heuristic; Cor 1 quadratic for RAM; Cor 2 slightly super-linear) via a length-monotonic depth-first traversal TRAVERSE_{c_Ω,c_Kt} that uses 1-Kt-randomness of Chaitin's Ω prefixes to bypass the black-box barrier. The technique relativizes and is **not subject to algebrization or Natural Proofs (T4)**, satisfying FOCUS-3's stated promise. **HOWEVER it does not extend to π(x) mod 2** for four orthogonal reasons: (O1) the hard string z that TRAVERSE produces is an oracle-dependent Kt-random prefix, NOT a fixed natural function — π_N is fixed per N and Brandt has no mechanism to use a "fast π-decider" to construct z; (O2) the contradiction is `Kt(z) ≥ |z|` ∧ `Kt(z) ≤ |M| + log₂ t` — self-referential Kt on both sides — and there is no analog of `f-hardness(z) ≥ |z|` for a fixed Boolean function f = π mod 2; (O3) Lemma 2's 1-Kt-randomness of Ω prefixes is the *only* black-box-barrier-bypass ingredient, and there is no density of "π-hard inputs" (π is total and in DTIME[x^{2/3}]); (O4) Brandt produces UNIFORM-TIME lower bounds, not circuit lower bounds — Chain E (E5.3) needs a circuit lower bound and Brandt explicitly avoids the Williams/Hirahara algorithmic-method route precisely because that's the route subject to Natural Proofs on stronger classes. Empirical pseudorandomness of π(x) mod 2 (E1.9, 33 measures, novel/pseudorandomness_of_pi.md) is consistent with π_N being Kt-random but does not establish it asymptotically and would itself face T4. Bounded-Kt simulator (3-bit-op stack VM, L_MAX=12, T_MAX=4096) confirms TRAVERSE behaves as in the paper (descends on Kt-random, right-steps on compressible) and that π_N is incompressible by short bounded-VM programs at all tested N ∈ {3..10}. Cumulative effect: with E7.10 (AKS modulus-twist orthogonality, S61/S64/S66) closing the AKS family and this closure ruling out Brandt-class diagonalisation-via-meta-complexity, **Chain E is now closed for both known technique families**; only an entirely-new lower-bound technique or a non-AKS TC⁰ primality test remains. Suggested EDGE: E5.8 — Brandt 2024 is structurally welded to MKtP. Cites E5.3, E5.5, E1.9, E7.10, T4. See experiments/constructions/brandt_mktp/. | 51 |
